rce, actor and period;"],[2984,"It covers the VA migrations only: an all-clear does not prove that the CA and KMS migration guards pass, so it does not replace the duplicate CA ID and `engine_id` checks described in PostgreSQL consolidation."],[2636,"It identifies `device-001`, supports client authentication and verifies against `ca.pem`."],[2634,"It is a CA certificate, is currently valid and its self-signature verifies."],[2442,"It requires the module, token and permissions of the corresponding provider. Check that the Lamassu identifier matches the object accessible from that module. Module configuration belongs to the client environment or Deployment."],[1497,"It sends the CA (in PEM) and the verification certificate to AWS via `iot:RegisterCACertificate`, enabling registration with `AllowAutoRegistration: true`."],[582,"It separately loads the policies granted to each principal."],[2517,"its chain reaches a root trusted by the consumer;"],[2518,"its dates and usages allow the operation;"],[2577,"Its public certificate in `ca.pem`; this example uses the root created in the previous path."],[2516,"its public key matches the client's private key;"],[2638,"Its public key matches the one included in `device.crt`."],[347,"Its status contains the expected address."],[2515,"its subject and extensions identify the expected client;"],[25,"Just want to evaluate the platform?"],[1511,"Just-In-Time Provisioning (JITP) mode delegates provisioning to AWS instead of the connector doing it directly. When the connector detects the DMS is in `jitp` mode, it creates a JITP provisioning template in AWS IoT Core and attaches it to the registered CA certificate. The connector attempts to create this template when the DMS is created and again on every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) — not at device enrollment time. The attempt is not idempotent: when a template named after the DMS ID already exists, the creation call fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in the `lamassu-dlq` poison queue (see Troubleshooting). The template is created once; later DMS edits never update its content. A DMS update that omits `metadata` or sends `null` keeps the stored metadata, so the connector key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}` remains present and the attempt is still triggered. A body that supplies a `metadata` object replaces the stored metadata entirely: if that object does not carry the connector key, the key is dropped and the connector silently skips the DMS's future events. Once the template is attached, when a device with a certificate issued by that CA connects to AWS IoT Core for the first time, AWS executes the template automatically: it creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it."],[282,"K3s"],[304,"K3s ServiceLB implements each `LoadBalancer` Service with pods that reserve the Service ports on the node. On a single node, only one Service can therefore own port 80 and one can own port 443. The default K3s Traefik Service may already hold both ports."],[687,"Keep `failOpen: false` unless a documented risk assessment justifies otherwise."],[266,"Keep `migrations.caToKms.enabled: true` for the upgrade that performs the migration, then remove the flag from your values once the migration completes; it defaults to `false`."],[977,"Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession."],[689,"Keep at least two recovery administrators and test their access periodically."],[2202,"Keep enough overlap to renew a disconnected fleet."],[1317,"Keep initial EST server trust separate from identity consumer trust."],[807,"keep the complete CloudEvent unmodified;"],[1760,"Keep the device identifier, owning DMS and serial number as references for the result."],[1212,"Keep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinated operation in the device store or configuration; avoid temporarily exposing a certificate with another version's key."],[2198,"Keep the root out of daily operations, or with very restricted access."],[2078,"Keep the serial number, issuer, revocation reason, query time and evidence timestamps. An existing session may need explicit closure or reauthentication in the consumer; publishing revocation does not automatically close it."],[298,"Keep these two settings separate:"],[2656,"Key"],[499,"Key (`primary_key`)"],[1769,"Key and certificate"],[830,"Key custody and cryptographic operations"],[1824,"Key custody, CA hierarchy, profiles and validation."],[2331,"Key inventory"],[1839,"Key rotation procedure"],[1983,"Key Usage and Extended Key Usage"],[2321,"Keys and cryptographic engines"],[797,"keys and KMS engines;"],[1800,"Keys, authorities, profiles, certificates and publication of revocation status."],[409,"KMS deployments using the native PKCS#11 sidecar require Kubernetes 1.29 or newer. Prebuilt Lamassu container images are published for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`, and the KMS PKCS#11 client module (`p11-kit-client.so`) is installed at the target architecture's Debian library path: `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on amd64 and `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on arm64. The x86\\_64 path is retained as a compatibility alias for existing configurations."],[2056,"KU, EKU and constraints permit the specific operation."],[403,"Kubernetes 1.24 or newer"],[2886,"Kubernetes derives a StatefulSet's PVC name from its pod name (`<volumeClaimTemplate>-<pod-name>`). Renaming the `kms` and `va` StatefulSets means `helm upgrade` binds brand-new, empty PVCs instead of the existing ones. The KMS key material and VA CRLs are not deleted — the old PVCs are orphaned, not removed — but the upgraded pods fail with `no such file or directory` (KMS) or `code=NotFound` (VA) until you copy the data across. This affects KMS when `services.kms.cryptoEngines` uses a `filesystem` engine and VA when `services.va.fileStore.type` is `local`. Every other renamed resource is stateless and is recreated without data-loss 