onent from needing direct access to private keys and lets each capability scale according to load."],[1862,"Lamassu does not allow reissuing an already-revoked CA or a CA whose expiration date has passed. A subordinate CA is signed by its parent, so the parent must also be `ACTIVE` and inside its validity period; otherwise the reissue fails with `400`. Plan ahead for these conditions."],[567,"Lamassu does not assign permissions directly to users. The full chain is:"],[724,"Lamassu evaluates the subscriptions associated with that event type."],[787,"Lamassu instruments the mutable operations of its services with audit events. Each event describes the operation, the principals that matched, the input, the result and whether it ended in error."],[1791,"Lamassu IoT lets you administer a private PKI and use it to issue, renew and revoke X.509 device identities. The Platform documentation follows two paths: **PKI**, for authorities and certificates, and **IoT Fleets**, for applying those identities to a device population."],[1778,"Lamassu IoT Platform"],[1977,"Lamassu keeps the certificate record of a deleted CA. If that same certificate is imported again, the import succeeds and reuses the record: it updates the type, issuer and key but keeps the status, so the CA reappears in the state it had when deleted (normally revoked or expired). A different certificate with the same serial number is rejected with a 400 error, and a certificate that already belongs to an existing CA with a 409 error. To avoid inheriting the previous status, use a certificate with a different serial number and check the result after importing. The reimported CA is registered under a new, backend-generated CA ID and does not inherit the CRL settings of the deleted CA: until those settings are deleted, those keep serving their last CRL at the CA ID URL, and at `/crl/<ski>` — the URL that certificates issued before the 4.0.0 upgrade carry — when the settings were migrated by that upgrade; when they are archived, they can no longer be edited (see CRL)."],[854,"Lamassu keeps the inventory, history and status of the identity."],[2139,"Lamassu persists four X.509 states:"],[1443,"Lamassu resource"],[54,"Lamassu SaaS offers the platform's capabilities in a managed environment. It is the right option when your team wants to focus on PKI and device identities, not on maintaining the infrastructure that supports them."],[1873,"Lamassu saves the CA’s revocation, then applies the same revocation to child CAs and issued certificates. Propagation continues recursively through child CAs. This is not a transaction over the whole hierarchy: some resources may have changed while other updates failed."],[367,"Lamassu services and Gateway API resources"],[2361,"Lamassu shows the enabled engines in the console."],[2256,"Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. Until Lamassu rejects imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA — proposed in lamassuiot#834, together with the per-CA CRL that 4.0.0 implements — restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with &#x2A;*Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same SKI collision, so avoid reusing keys across CAs."],[575,"Lamassu uses two authorization paths depending on who implements the protected service. They share principals, policies and grants, but they do not interpret the rules the same way."],[2690,"lamassu-dashboard"],[2691,"lamassu-helm"],[1328,"Lamassu's connector for AWS IoT Core syncs device identities with the AWS platform, handling CA registration, Thing provisioning and certificate lifecycle automatically and event-driven."],[1354,"Lamassu's integration with AWS IoT Core bridges the PKI managed by Lamassu and AWS's connectivity platform. When a device obtains its certificate through the EST enrollment process, the connector ensures that certificate