r an operator assigns it an identity."],[2161,"The device history relates its previous and current identities. For administrative changes and mutation errors, see Audit logs."],[2182,"The device installs the certificate and the chain it needs."],[1543,"The device must present both its certificate and the issuing CA's certificate in the TLS handshake with AWS IoT Core."],[2179,"The device proves it can enroll according to the DMS policy."],[2144,"The device view may add operational states such as missing identity, pending renewal, expiring soon or decommissioned. These are an interpretation of the device's status and its certificates, not new X.509 states."],[1624,"The device's association with its certificates: an active version and a history."],[2177,"The DMS acts as the enrollment point. It can authenticate an EST request with a client certificate, an external webhook or a combination of both. CAs configured to validate the requester do not have to be the CA that will issue the new identity."],[1879,"The DMS delivers the correct chain through EST `/cacerts`."],[936,"The DMS first looks up the presented certificate in Lamassu; certificates it issued and manages are checked against their stored status. External certificates are checked through their OCSP servers and then their CRL distribution points. By default, unverifiable revocation is a rejection, not an acceptance: a certificate that is not managed by Lamassu and lists no OCSP or CRL endpoint is rejected unless `allow_unverifiable_revocation` is set in that operation's block (see Authentication per operation)."],[1315,"The DMS may include the available downstream TLS certificate, selected Managed CAs and enrollment CA. Selecting the issuer does not include every ancestor. The set may contain leaves, roots and intermediates, including multiple hierarchies during migration."],[930,"The DMS sends operation context to the configured service. Method comes from configuration; agree the contract before selecting POST